  passanPlan: { premise:'ESPN / Jeff Passan (2019): The MLB draft is structurally broken — it rewards losing, punishes development investment, and fails to create a true market for amateur talent.', howItWorks:['Eliminate draft entirely for domestic amateurs — all players become free agents at age 16-18.','Implement hard international spending pools for all amateurs (domestic + international unified market).','Revenue sharing tied to player development success metrics, not draft position.','Competitive balance preserved through luxury tax escalation, not draft order.'], legalStatus:'Viable under CBA — draft structure is collectively bargained, not statutory. Requires MLBPA and owners to agree.', tradeoff:'True free market for amateurs eliminates tanking incentive but creates financial arms race where wealthy franchises dominate amateur market.', discussed:'Discussed in 2022 CBA negotiations — not adopted. International pool reforms partially address the issue.' },
  draftEfficiency: { definition:'Nandakumar & Wharton (2017): A "perfect draft" is one where each pick selected was the best available player at that position in the draft class, in hindsight.', methodology:'Compare actual selections against player career value (WAR or points equivalent). Calculate % of picks that were the best available at each slot.', efficiencyMetric:'Draft Efficiency % = (Picks that were best available) / (Total picks). League average is ~18-22% — most teams do dramatically worse than chance would suggest.', mlbApplication:'Teams with strong scouting infrastructure (Cardinals, Dodgers, Rays) consistently show higher draft efficiency over 10-year windows. Analytics integration correlates with improved efficiency.', keyInsight:'Draft efficiency improves most dramatically in rounds 3-10 — round 1 is adequately scouted by all teams. The edge is in identifying late-round value where information asymmetry is highest.', nhFinding:'NHL study found franchises in the bottom quartile of draft efficiency consistently missed playoffs regardless of other roster construction decisions — draft is a long-term talent lever, not a short-term fix.' },
  scoutingFramework: { oldSchool:{ philosophy:'Project current tools to peak — raw athleticism, physicality, and measurable tools (velocity, exit velocity) are primary. Character and makeup are heavy qualitative filters.', tools:['Hit tool (contact ability)','Power (raw and game)','Run (speed)','Arm (strength and accuracy)','Field (glove and range)'] }, moneyballModel:{ philosophy:'College players over HS — lower variance, shorter development timeline, better statistical track record to evaluate. OBP and plate discipline over traditional tools.', limitations:'Ignores ceiling — college safe picks cluster in the middle of career WAR distributions. High-ceiling HS picks dominate the top of all-time WAR lists.' }, modernModel:{ approach:'Hybrid: Statcast measurables (bat speed, arm strength via Trackman, spin rate) + traditional scouting + college statistical quality adjustment. Aims to identify ceiling while reducing bust rate.', riskDimension:'College vs HS is now a risk-adjusted return question, not a binary preference. Teams model expected WAR distribution, not just expected WAR.' } },
  strategies: { bestPlayerAvailable:'Draft the highest-graded player regardless of position need. Maximizes expected draft class WAR. Optimal in most models.', positionOfNeed:'Draft for organizational roster gaps. Sub-optimal in WAR terms but can accelerate MLB-ready timelines for contending teams.', signabilityAdjusted:'Shade grades downward for players with high slot demands or college commitment leverage. Maximizes expected player signed per pool dollar.', internationalArbitrage:'Spend less on domestic draft (take signable players at slots), redirect savings toward IFA market where dollar-per-WAR is historically higher.', pitchingHeavy:'Load on arms early — pitching development is the primary organizational lever. Pitching scarcity at MLB level means pitcher WAR commands premium.' },
  bargainingFindings: { finding1:'Teams systematically underpay picks 1-10 relative to their true value — slot system compresses compensation for elite talent.', finding2:'HS players 3-5% more likely to reach MLB when drafted by teams with strong pitching development infrastructure — organization matters for development outcome.', finding3:'College players drafted in rounds 2-5 show highest dollar-per-WAR efficiency — late-round college value is the most persistent market inefficiency in the draft.', implication:'The optimal draft strategy combines: (1) best player available in round 1, (2) over-slot HS upside in rounds 2-3 with saved pool dollars, (3) college safe picks in rounds 4-10 for organizational depth.' },
